# Conventional Commits Specification
# Format: <type>(<scope>): <short summary>
# Separate header and body with a blank line
# Separate body and footer with a blank line

# Allowed Commit Types:
# feat:     A new feature
# fix:      A bug fix
# docs:     Documentation only changes
# style:    Code formatting, no logic modification
# refactor: Code refactoring, no bug fix / no new feature
# perf:     Performance improvement
# test:     Add or modify test cases
# chore:    Build, tooling, dependency & routine maintenance
# ci:       CI/CD workflow and config changes
# build:    Build system or external dependencies changes
# revert:   Revert a prior commit

# Scope (optional):
# Specify business module, component, service or directory

# Short Summary Rules:
# - Use imperative mood
# - Max 50 characters
# - No trailing period

# Body (optional):
# - Explain the motivation and details of changes
# - Wrap text at 72 characters per line
# - Describe what & why, not how

# Footer Section (optional):
# 1. Issue linkage: Fixes #123 / Closes #456 / Resolves #789
# 2. Breaking Change: BREAKING CHANGE: <description>
# 3. Co-author signature for pair programming:
#    Co-authored-by: Name <email@example.com>


# ---------------------- Commit Example ----------------------
# feat(api): add request rate limiter middleware
#
# Prevent excessive requests to protect server stability,
# support dynamic threshold configuration.
#
# Closes #201
# Co-authored-by: Tom Lee <tom@company.com>
# ------------------------------------------------------------
